WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:03.965
Hello everyone. This is The English

00:00:03.965 --> 00:00:05.920
We Speak with me, Feifei...

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.739
... and me, Rob. Hey, Feifei, do you

00:00:08.739 --> 00:00:11.010
need me to order you a pizza?

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.279
Why would I want you to order me a pizza

00:00:14.279 --> 00:00:17.140
when we are presenting a programme?

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.322
Go on! Just say 'pizza' and my new

00:00:20.322 --> 00:00:22.716
smartphone will instantly

00:00:22.716 --> 00:00:24.440
order one for you.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.680
Oh, I see. I know you've got a new and

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.941
very expensive smartphone,

00:00:29.941 --> 00:00:32.029
Rob, but I'm quite happy

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:35.320
using my bog-standard dumbphone.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.131
'Bog-standard'! You mean it's basic,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.240
ordinary and unexceptional?

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.420
I do, Rob. It's a strange expression

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.070
and nobody really knows its origins.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.217
Well, I think it's got something to do with

00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:52.290
the English slang for toilet - bog - which

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.730
is where your 'old' phone should be!

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.180
Thanks, Rob. Shall we hear some

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.400
'bog-standard' examples now?

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.080
I've got a bog-standard computer, but it's

00:01:04.082 --> 00:01:05.591
fine for just sending emails

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:06.400
and surfing the

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.540
internet.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.440
Sorry you got a low mark in your

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.780
homework, but to be honest,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:12.960
it was bog-standard.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.020
I found the new film bog-standard - it

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.340
lacked the action and

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.180
special effects I was expecting.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.060
This is The English We Speak from BBC

00:01:25.072 --> 00:01:26.769
Learning English, and we're

00:01:26.769 --> 00:01:28.340
learning about the phrase

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.675
'bog-standard', which describes

00:01:30.675 --> 00:01:33.057
things that are basic, ordinary

00:01:33.057 --> 00:01:34.440
and unexceptional.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.400
Yes, like your basic mobile phone.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.220
Oh, is that a message on your

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.660
new shiny, very expensive, smartphone?

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.640
Yes, yes, it is. It's my first bill for my

00:01:47.654 --> 00:01:50.356
new phone.... Let's have a look.

00:01:50.356 --> 00:01:51.200
What?! How

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.000
much! Oh!

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.600
Oh dear, Rob. It's not such a new shiny

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.600
phone now, is it?

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.092
Oh no, how are we going to order

00:02:02.092 --> 00:02:03.020
a pizza now?

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.292
On my 'bog-standard' phone? Here, just

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.485
press the numbers and you

00:02:08.485 --> 00:02:11.029
can actually talk to someone.

00:02:11.029 --> 00:02:12.940
No, I'm not hungry now.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:13.920
Bye!

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.080
Bye!

